A||||10||yellowFormula||Stereo question
Z||000000||yellowFormula||05-16-99||04:36 PM||||OK this question is for people who have removed the triangular piece of metal with a hole in it to install a radio deeper then 6", how the heck did you get it off, and what tools did you use?  Oh yeah I do not want to remove the dash....
Z||000001||Eric||05-16-99||06:47 PM||||I know what you're talking about,yF.  I just had the dash off today (repainting it).  I saw that piece of metal you mentioned.  I think the mounting depth with the piece installed is about 5 3/4 inches, most radio are about 6 1/2 inches.  I don't see any reason why you couldn't take the center consol cover off, take the radio out, and use a small cutting tool, like a dremel, to remove the metal bracket.<p>I don't know if you have an aftermarket radio already, but if you don't you should consider Pioneer's DEH-45DH.  It's a little more expensive, but the fit and finish is a lot better.
Z||000002||yellowFormula||05-16-99||08:32 PM||||Thanks for the advice eric, I had considered using my dremel and that is what I will probably end up doing.  I had considered buying a Pionner DEH-46Dh or PEH-P76DH,DEH-P86DHR,(all perfect fits in Fiero's) but I need 3 preamp outputs and all 3 models only had two, and for the features they have I feel they were too expensive, so I ordered the Sony Mobile ES CDX-7850.  I have yet to get it installed due to that annoying piece of worthless metal, and as I said before I guess the dremel will have to take care of it...     
Z||000003||FieroMike||05-17-99||10:00 AM||||I used a hacksaw blad, it went right through, then sanded it down with a dremel.
Z||000004||Eric||05-17-99||11:51 AM||||Fiero Mike,<p>Could you tell me how the radio looks in the dash.  I was considering buying one of the GM Chrysler fit receivers, but like YF said, they don't have the features for the money.  I was a little concerned about radio sticking out of the dash too much, but it seems like you have resolved this problem.  Do the custom kits, places like Crutchfield offer, look like a factory install? 
Z||000005||Patrick||05-17-99||02:05 PM||||<br>Hey guys, if I remember correctly from when I had everything apart, "<b>that annoying piece of worthless metal</b>" is actually there to support the weight of the radio that's installed. There's a "knob" that sticks out from the <b>back</b> of the radio that goes into a hole in this bracket. Unless you plan to support the weight of the radio with some other apparatus, you might be "stressing" the front mount if you only rely on that. Remember, we're not discussing mounting something that's sitting still in our living rooms at home. Anything that's mounted in a moving vehicle needs to be properly supported.<p>When I got rid of the crappy stock radio in my Fiero, I replaced it with a higher quality "old" style unit that has the control dials on each side. Of course, this wouldn't mount properly without an adapter. The big advantage of using this adapter is that you have complete control over how far back the radio has to go before a supplied and installed "knob" engages with "<b>that annoying piece of worthless metal</b>". This application doesn't look absolutely stock, but the radio and adapter combination only sticks out a half inch or so and it looks great. Only a Fiero "expert" would know that anything has been altered. And it's never going to move!<p><p><br>
Z||000006||yellowFormula||05-17-99||07:59 PM||||Patrick, I had considered if it was improtant or not but I talked to a crutchfield tech guy and he said that the free kit they gvie you has a support for that reason so that will take care of that.
Z||000007||yellowFormula||05-17-99||08:02 PM||||Patrick, I had wondered if that "annoying piece of worthless metal" was necessary for anything but I talked to a Crutchfield tech guy and he said that the free kit they send has supports included, so that will take car of that...
Z||000008||yellowFormula||05-17-99||08:31 PM||||oops, sorry about the double post guys! I didn't think the first one went through! oh well...
Z||000009||Patrick||05-17-99||09:17 PM||||<br>I don't know anything about the Crutchfield kit, but if it includes some kind of a support that doesn't require "that annoying piece of worthless metal", great, get out the hacksaw. The important thing is that the radio is supported somehow.<p> 
Z||000010||FieroMike||05-18-99||11:33 AM||||Eric,<br>Depends on what deck you get.  I have a Kenwood KDC series, and with the bracket removed, almost any DIN style radio is flush with the front.  The bracket IS there to support the back of the stock stereo, but most kits come with new brackets for the rear.  Like the Kenwood.  I think it looks very nice.  I centered mine in the middle and it doesn't look out of proportion!  Have fun.  If U need any help with installing it e-mail me.
